#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175711 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "e4f2ef63-cc21-5aeb-b1c4-6a7a4d40e81a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12411" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1eecba17a7af16086022541eaab52f0755f959cb3f0e5059d61322bbbd81c9c4.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=25$ for $y \\geq 0$ and $z \\leq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/94/9415dd50c82e3f5f4e47e26f459dda1315f819348e87ff97a4b05b1c27b59891.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}2,\\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}, \\: \n{0} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\pi}, \\: \n \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}2} \\leq \\phi \\leq {\\pi}   \n\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "68b95ae7-c0ac-590c-a764-0500455ca70c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12412" :question/problem "![](images/04/0437b193a24fadb4e43ec449e685abd9b8a220ff72019d6c5b1d242e6eb49942.png)\n\n \n\nUse spherical polar coordinates to describe the solid enclosed by the hemisphere $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ for $x \\geq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents a half-sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,0,-2).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2+y^2+z^2+4z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +4\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho = -4\\cos\\phi.$\n\n![](images/44/449c0a320c59fc072c7ca38307d9929910b1dfa432d7e3afdf11824e97270dc5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-4\\cos\\phi]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac\\pi 2,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\n- $\\phi \\in \\left[\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pi\\right]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, \n0 \\leq \\rho \\leq -4\\cos\\phi, \\: \n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\: \n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq  \\pi\n\\right\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175816 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/id #uuid "78981c7f-ec42-5eac-afb8-145bd2a7e66c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8811" :question/problem "What is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?\n\n![](images/a6/a60ddaebcf32cd8d69624b79f34d96d8ca1c18ec59c8bc443a95e235dedfae9e.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho$ and $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\phi = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175817 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "bc7cbddf-7c15-5762-b035-cbd68a1f25f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8812" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho=5$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho=5\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $5.$ On the other hand, $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere of radius $5.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a8c82e617e1d040c61f0f7fca8dd35985005df553aaa53b95e4b14871df6d8b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ade53df-f047-5ea9-ad5f-64f2b44fc397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532055} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd7221e-4681-539c-8b73-08fedb80fbbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532139} {:answer/id #uuid "65926222-59a9-5c6b-8e18-d6118f338b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{\\pi }{3}ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532157} {:answer/id #uuid "6d48c000-d46c-5620-896f-57c4438decc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532182} {:answer/id #uuid "d7436a96-8f7a-563d-8a2e-27bd76bd83af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7436a96-8f7a-563d-8a2e-27bd76bd83af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532393} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03fafa7-5de5-5b87-8159-28addb0e0b3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52ca5532-94f1-527a-af84-d4e9f2c326d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-102943" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae59540093c131582cf3d7c682a093eea6cbced9d8c88975a925e5c1d71123f9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25770517-4a8b-58a6-aebe-d00c58c307b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236059} {:answer/id #uuid "47637a16-4