tance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $2.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3393b1f7-6a5f-5ed8-8699-113fb09e64fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =3\\rho " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266596} {:answer/id #uuid "438b6d53-0463-5cb5-be14-303ecf520e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266748} {:answer/id #uuid "aa516819-50e0-5f63-8a35-e7eab0c258c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267683} {:answer/id #uuid "e288cb29-8e2c-5349-a21d-f6d13deaa8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268145} {:answer/id #uuid "e83396d8-4c0d-56db-bda1-c9d30f58b101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =9\\rho " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e288cb29-8e2c-5349-a21d-f6d13deaa8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268145} :answer-field/id #uuid "965d8e4a-1bae-59f6-ac0c-fcb414500ba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48cc5ea4-9288-5e4d-b708-8718d21e8b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-135247" :question/problem "![](images/db/db4700a08fd81ab6d2bc07d80d72fb1fc9edea6c0cdf652b251a755b53a2b148.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $3.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5860-9b89-16b59c5613bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533da413-4cc6-5072-bfd4-5130aa7b1b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532113} {:answer/id #uuid "95019874-835f-5630-8478-7510f7ff01d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532272} {:answer/id #uuid "d379e0ab-40d7-5be4-a02e-62f11a49df2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\rho =\\frac{\\theta }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532386} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f9704c-1b98-5e60-a1d1-c90ca30c1a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532397} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f36413-282b-594b-bf19-b41c6ca3f9d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "533da413-4cc6-5072-bfd4-5130aa7b1b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532113} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d22858-0f2e-526e-bced-96022129988c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "554c2eb9-1068-5995-9e2b-d384110d6d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-135255" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c923ff3fd6a74263ed05a0ace025e05b0e1b5c445c2bfb0801b8707ce9ac356.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175966 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "133f6b90-4074-5