ed-solution "In the picture, we are given a half-cone with the $z$-axis as its axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given half-cone makes an angle of $\\frac{8\\pi }{9}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $ϕ=\\frac{8\\pi }{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e47c97e-55dc-53c8-9711-65d1b9fc5ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532025} {:answer/id #uuid "2fed5099-caf7-543c-92f2-49d96f84c06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532031} {:answer/id #uuid "31aa13bc-974f-57ab-9747-45bd823fbe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532034} {:answer/id #uuid "a698c164-580d-54d0-9ef9-6f4e0a0b42c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532310} {:answer/id #uuid "b7434448-9bab-5c7b-8c21-bca12a9fa684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7434448-9bab-5c7b-8c21-bca12a9fa684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532334} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f41120f-43ad-516f-be5d-f4fc7aad71f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f975b4e0-ae07-50ae-a2a8-6fe6de3927cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-135297" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =1$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =1\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $1.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](images/6b/6bca743f355735dae56d679cc601852f5a52bd5cc99e89a8d4f20a92111cf621.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f251965-fc78-59a5-84e4-de545ef05e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266414} {:answer/id #uuid "48261a0e-dcbe-5690-861d-4415470d5467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266789} {:answer/id #uuid "76c2155d-f7dc-5291-9fbd-a97616a5e56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267186} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3ae1a7-9ccb-5689-9542-21fd7fd44e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267569} {:answer/id #uuid "de07bfc7-2d59-5f36-b8fd-e6688190600f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76c2155d-f7dc-5291-9fbd-a97616a5e56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267186} :answer-field/id #uuid "64428624-3fb4-579f-a7fa-f7a2a1ff7711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268588}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c6c5a6f-f486-57c2-a5c5-e573d7067374" :question/math-academy-id "q-135300" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\rho =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the distance from the point to the origin must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is a sphere.\n\n![](images/37/3775fdcb48fd015097b484b6d5ebaefcecd4ef10298cb18b34226a8eae1be1ef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32461572-55bc-5f57-b231-fb17b4eaa812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532036} {:answer/id #uuid "3c27e55e-48dd-567a-b8e9-f381889e732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532059} {:answer/id #uuid "3d59c90c-9f40-57a5-8e98-bfd01f70ea85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperboloid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532062} {:answer/id #uuid "479a1efc-6aee-5db2-81fe-fdda094e2d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532077} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b9ed22-ede1-58b1-b1b0-d97a6798e3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b9ed22-ede1-58b1-b1b0-d97a6798e3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532353} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d19a91-9b5e-5bde-be4d-876637ef2257" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "151bdd66-5085-5cc9-a9af-25457fe9b069" :question/math-academy-id "q-135301" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\pi $ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\pi \n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $\\pi .$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/41/415596385e885cf6c8fb553d126c550a78879d3503267bae102a8e038a688eb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "196d1c20-1305-5f81-a984-4e2fc6ded6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circular cylinder" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266352} {:answer/id #uuid "472a0bb2-8925-5776-8dbe-90c5f4e739ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-line" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266777} {:answer/id #uuid "74f779d8-62a1-57c6-a0f5-7e10d8eb5f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.t