ctive domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/00/0043ffdc885e7edeebb7c4df571466fe4f13dfb08728290cd6d54f47777a7179.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175967 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a52e1346-75c4-537c-add9-777911dbe5ff" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Spherical Representation of a Surface From an Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08cc1fc9-61ac-5985-8f64-58da4f35ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-cone" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266224} {:answer/id #uuid "58939d3a-1b18-5461-ba93-b671b729d8f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266936} {:answer/id #uuid "e652bd2c-6d8c-5ba6-826a-b7fa9c10673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268186} {:answer/id #uuid "e9768716-f4a2-5c1e-b63c-0b7b884e5909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-sphere" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268221} {:answer/id #uuid "ec616c23-9f10-552e-b69e-3ca66e74c9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e652bd2c-6d8c-5ba6-826a-b7fa9c10673a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A half-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268186} :answer-field/id #uuid "a88266da-0d3f-56f5-89ed-ba11cda724aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268707}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89a2bbd2-2dd6-5b47-be2c-faba4acc741a" :question/math-academy-id "q-135303" :question/problem "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =0$ in spherical polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =0\n$$\n\nThis equation specifies that for any point on the surface, the angle that the corresponding vector makes with the positive $x$-axis must equal $0.$ On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $ϕ$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\ge 0,$ our surface is a half-plane.\n\n![](images/83/832655d3a5ee1429fb1e1c005faa8c19d2a92ecfcf00cef64cfb165bf4755729.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c99e65e-6e27-5a52-a1ef-cf4d95ddd8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532020} {:answer/id #uuid "4c385d42-4832-5a3c-8b1d-ff343c8165fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532091} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca9a438-788a-5b25-a87d-38318a835227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532288} {:answer/id #uuid "9d030973-0753-54b0-87b4-0a1ff3984ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532290} {:answer/id #uuid "b17869dd-a218-5ca1-b711-91b1d2c490b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca9a438-788a-5b25-a87d-38318a835227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532288} :answer-field/id #uuid "b21e68ec-d30d-5b80-bad7-bc3780fa39d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3cde71-1b72-5270-99f3-cec1724b7c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161732" :question/problem "![](images/33/3358dd177a848ea569fdeab6e39f4be268cc7325bb01c788e9bfe2ea2145d1eb.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e5/e5371e711515feccdaab3f6b10734fb35bb59c724fc408c8b67b4b54132136ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,5]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5088423b-db3e-54df-b831-a57602e5ebe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532107} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd62de5-f0c5-582e-9f22-e1174588775b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532176} {:answer/id #uuid "805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532224} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd5bc74-b990-585b-b3cb-9771824e5c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532345} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e6ef4b-e544-53c2-99a8-afb161df11b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "805a0a6c-588a-5652-8708-ef6f81947bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532224} :answer-field/id #uuid "acc82db6-7cde-56cc-80d8-b767a5862ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f229d07-8a86-5591-a00d-4cf4336bfb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-161736" :question/problem "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/3a/3a6985012edbaf28ca42d88fd47e3769fa53704cad8f8c09021c944d966a3d48.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa7ac06-8f23-52b1-a003-b4d31f99965a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531961} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcacf41-075b-588f-927e-47fb253864c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532175} {:answer/id #uuid "7b887772-b201-5bfa-90a5-89c7bb297020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532210} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcbbfdf-efed-5827-9f40-6d1768ae8a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532341} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f04e7a-6ee7-574b-9acd-39ccfd7fa0e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bcacf41-075b-588f-927e-47fb253864c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532175} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7ce485-b829-5a13-88f2-198fc5306c35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a536dd94-d69c-5fc7-af99-867a1849acf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161821" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd321d5afaa151a8da5caa6f4ca561f93566e35ff87235c06854b56cf52c0c69.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the hemisphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/43/437d96d18823f97c824fe7dfe353120d0c297bdac3f264530e615905afff8d58.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:  \n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ef2694-93c9-5433-b86a-aa9f99a0bde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532000} {:answer/id #uuid "4220d81b-5fee-5b30-8b21-5a4d1398318f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532070} {:answer/id #uuid "5962da49-0814-5ce2-838a-0da88eb72219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532128} {:answer/id #uuid "895ec483-a133-5e14-a169-801e1f3a0cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532245} {:answer/id #uuid "e6783016-1b3a-5048-9e49-5135f2d7cdae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6783016-1b3a-5048-9e49-5135f2d7cdae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532427} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84278ed-800b-5b51-9093-4a9b24f617ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeaaefb9-3b47-501a-8362-26f6341a17d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161823" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d829672b7920642fec44fb79822dcee2c45bddd7298189f2c04e24b31f2477b.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $y\\ge 0$ and $x\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/0d/0d901312b6a14c863085276458cf8341cbd4cb6cc5411d5c5045244b8c656eb2.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4faf5c03-6510-5ce8-addf-575bb84d8f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 12,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /6\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532105} {:answer/id #uuid "608156a9-23aa-57d6-b271-1637a899487d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532146} {:answer/id #uuid "720b34ff-283b-595f-8f21-a06e203e3f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532189} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1d01c9-8cd3-51c4-9e7c-da21a084bab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /4\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532338} {:answer/id #uuid "f4ba179b-d2b8-58c4-9e9b-66578364394c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 36,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi /2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "608156a9-23aa-57d6-b271-1637a899487d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\p