a129dbdc121e20bcfdb7f8d0d443.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/cc/cca15d8d91ebfe643777122cf41a49f2bfe3bac901b30e160cad65eb6b4b102f.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary withing the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ac36fc6-724d-59ba-bc41-3af29a3a8de8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532054} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c646fb-4131-53cd-a9c8-3a4ae803fde4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532297} {:answer/id #uuid "c9301d6b-61d0-5484-9019-19d7b668ce0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le 2\\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532367} {:answer/id #uuid "cb387986-8ebd-5bb9-877c-9abb1c25a852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532371} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6d2993-fd8c-52ec-92fb-7d3841d36a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea6d2993-fd8c-52ec-92fb-7d3841d36a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532439} :answer-field/id #uuid "a25a1262-2d6c-5d10-9f4a-f4ca15577a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7ea971b-d67e-5a60-86aa-35a475db9053" :question/math-academy-id "q-161851" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d829e8f51e5cd49bf20587f0cd70abd29a8dd1a336a6bad827541726c9cd4416.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a74817d9d1f9cb581.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0452cb-7133-5cf9-bafe-72ae2c543e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531994} {:answer/id #uuid "821c59c1-9fae-533c-87a2-1c8d423ee015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532227} {:answer/id #uuid "8f75f27a-bcb9-5bed-b305-7616971ca23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532258} {:answer/id #uuid "e8643e12-d858-53e7-9f58-5292e345b9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532435} {:answer/id #uuid "f98a9165-7703-5823-a7ab-afca420773e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0452cb-7133-5cf9-bafe-72ae2c543e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531994} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef5564a-71c2-51d9-a5c5-1e430a20af03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b4d1e3e-36ae-5b22-9e31-1455caee6c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-161852" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b15c12a1cabbab36feab2d15e1ac3d73e8d8c26afc14a8b820e2ca6f57d7d48d.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2z,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0,1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/66/66812de081c1bbfe515162ab628806717b5602ec1a6cc61eaf22514f91e9c0ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ca9eaa8-0cf0-5859-b374-fdd5806c8ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532021} {:answer/id #uuid "722d91c6-40fa-56a4-867b-919bace76541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532190} {:answer/id #uuid "a973e4e0-ee5f-5fc6-ab29-b94e84360208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532315} {:answer/id #uuid "da839e87-16a6-50ac-87fc-5f547394b48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532402} {:answer/id #uuid "ec725f35-2519-51f8-8cc0-4a11c90e6631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec725f35-2519-51f8-8cc0-4a11c90e6631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532445} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c562141-9c96-531d-9229-9444719807a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "507eb77e-f4ca-5d1f-88a7-ae46f550b999" :question/math-academy-id "q-161881" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9ae8e3cb2b2554ec3bff9a4912cdca39d12acbfd7dcb93bf44248a35dccc3dfa.png)\n\nThe solid enclosed by the quarter-sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z=0$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ shown above, can be described in spherical polar coordinates as" :question/worked-solution "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(0,0,-1).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z=0$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z & =0 \\\\ {\\rho }^{2}+2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & =0 \\\\ \\rho (\\rho +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/14/1499d492c526bad77e81aca1128b6160cc574a209ad7706e8d89b3012112e1df.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that inside our region, the spherical polar coordinates vary within the following domains:\n\n- $\\rho \\in [0,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]$\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\n- $ϕ\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0888c7a6-5da3-5a1f-b87d-986ab2864e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531954} {:answer/id #uuid "18faa7fa-7eb2-59fb-8bb6-a8207578ac26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531983} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9d4d58-b589-531c-a608-5dd3c04d9134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532060} {:answer/id #uuid "7f98c60e-4e49-520e-a5f5-776539a57fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532221} {:answer/id #uuid "f3af3470-369d-504e-8f80-0f77cd8a8f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f98c60e-4e49-520e-a5f5-776539a57fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\{(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le ϕ\\le \\pi \\}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532221} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f220f6-dfc0-5aa2-a15f-43b1a7d6dfc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c94b1ab-44af-5e40-9fea-2f1ea696e7ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-161883" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a32e8fd24c990a831cfee9525c6b1c0